                "v": "<strong>Compensation: 2/5.</strong> Below-median positioning, and the dominant reason people who do leave give is finding a better offer — a direct, repeated pay signal.<br><br><strong>The key insight is the gap between turnover and engagement.</strong> Low turnover (14%) alongside low engagement (+6 eNPS) is a classic sign of <em>disengaged retention</em> — people who aren't actively looking but aren't genuinely committed either. The stay-interview comment ('I'd leave if something better came along') confirms this directly. A company can look healthy on a turnover dashboard while quietly accumulating exactly the kind of flight risk that shows up the moment the labour market loosens or a competitor starts hiring aggressively.<br><br>This is why turnover alone is a lagging, sometimes misleading metric — engagement data caught a risk that the headline retention number was actively hiding."
