            "id": "1-1-3",
            "title": "Nova Tech's Retention Crisis — Full Case Analysis",
            "duration": "12 min",
            "xp": 150,
            "type": "lesson",
            "body": [
              {
                "t": "intro",
                "v": "Single-paragraph scenarios are useful for illustrating a concept. Real diagnostic work requires sitting with more data and more ambiguity. This case gives you that fuller picture."
              },
              {
                "t": "scenario",
                "label": "Case Background",
                "title": "Nova Tech",
                "v": "Nova Tech is a 140-person SaaS company, three years past its Series B. Engineering headcount has nearly doubled in 18 months. The VP of Engineering has flagged that senior engineers — the people Nova Tech can least afford to lose — are leaving at almost twice the rate of junior engineers."
              },
              {
                "t": "box",
                "label": "The Data",
                "v": "Senior engineer turnover (18 months): 31%\nJunior engineer turnover (18 months): 16%\nAverage senior engineer tenure before leaving: 14 months\nMedian counter-offer accepted by leavers: 4% above current pay\nExit survey: ‘lack of technical growth path’ cited by 78% of senior leavers\nExit survey: ‘manager doesn't understand my work deeply enough to grow me’ cited by 61%"
              },
              {
                "t": "h",
                "v": "Discussion Point 1"
              },
              {
                "t": "p",
                "v": "Before reading further: if you were advising Nova Tech's CHRO, what would you investigate first, and why?"
              },
              {
                "t": "reveal",
                "label": "See how an experienced practitioner would approach this",
                "v": "The 4% counter-offer detail is the single most important data point here. If pay were the real driver, you'd expect senior engineers to be chasing 15–25% pay jumps elsewhere — that's the typical gap needed to justify the disruption of leaving a stable role. A 4% gap accepted as ‘good enough to leave for’ tells you the job itself, not the paycheck, is what's pushing them out the door."
              },
              {
                "t": "h",
                "v": "Discussion Point 2"
              },
              {
                "t": "p",
                "v": "The exit survey shows two related but distinct complaints: lack of a growth path, and managers who don't understand the work deeply enough to develop people. Which would you tackle first, and why?"
              },
              {
                "t": "reveal",
                "label": "See the reasoning",
                "v": "Manager capability is usually the faster, higher-leverage fix. A formal technical career ladder takes months to design and calibrate fairly. Training senior managers — many promoted for technical skill, not people development — to run substantive growth conversations can start within weeks and directly addresses the second complaint while a longer-term ladder is built."
              },
              {
                "t": "h",
                "v": "Discussion Point 3"
              },
              {
                "t": "p",
                "v": "Nova Tech's CFO proposes a blanket 10% raise for all senior engineers as a quick retention fix. What's your honest assessment of this proposal?"
              },
              {
                "t": "reveal",
                "label": "See the reasoning",
                "v": "It would likely buy a few months of goodwill but wouldn't resolve the underlying issue — and it sets a precedent that future retention problems get solved with cash, which is the most expensive lever available and the one Nova Tech has the least flexibility to keep pulling. The data suggests this money would be better spent building the technical ladder and manager capability the exit surveys are actually pointing to."
              },
              {
                "t": "h",
                "v": "Discussion Point 4"
              },
              {
                "t": "p",
                "v": "Six months later, assume Nova Tech has built a technical ladder and trained managers. Senior turnover has dropped to 19%. What would you check next to confirm this was the actual cause, rather than coincidence?"
              },
              {
                "t": "reveal",
                "label": "See the reasoning",
                "v": "Check whether exit survey themes among the remaining leavers have shifted — if ‘lack of growth path’ has dropped sharply as a cited reason while other themes (workload, pay, location) have become relatively more prominent, that's strong evidence the intervention worked. If the same themes persist among the smaller group still leaving, the drop in turnover may be coincidental — a hiring slowdown elsewhere in the market, for instance — and the underlying issue hasn't actually been fixed."
              },
              {
                "t": "h",
                "v": "Discussion Point 5"
              },
              {
                "t": "p",
                "v": "Nova Tech's VP of Engineering wants to roll the same technical-ladder-plus-manager-training fix out to the Product and Design teams, who have entirely different attrition patterns and exit themes. Is this a good idea?"
              },
              {
                "t": "reveal",
                "label": "See the reasoning",
                "v": "Not without first running the same diagnostic on Product and Design specifically. The fix that worked for Engineering was correctly targeted because it was built from Engineering's specific data — the 4% counter-offer detail, the dominance of growth-path and manager-depth complaints. Applying the same solution to a different function, with potentially different drivers, repeats the exact mistake the case opened with: reaching for a familiar fix before diagnosing the actual problem. The right move is to run the Five Pillars Diagnostic on Product and Design's own data before assuming the same intervention applies."
              },
              {
                "t": "takeaways",
                "items": [
                  "A small accepted counter-offer is one of the clearest signals that pay isn't the real driver of attrition.",
                  "Manager capability gaps are often a faster, higher-leverage fix than building a formal structural programme from scratch.",
                  "A blanket pay increase used as a retention fix sets an expensive precedent without addressing root causes.",
                  "Confirming an intervention worked requires checking whether the underlying signal shifted — not just whether the headline turnover number improved.",
                  "A fix that worked for one team shouldn't be assumed to transfer to a different team without re-running the diagnostic on that team's own data."
                ]
              }
            ]
